{"qid":"Q704300","names":["Free City of Frankfurt"],"title":"Free City of Frankfurt","endonym":{"native":"Freie Stadt Frankfurt","lang":"de"},"capsule":"The Free City of Frankfurt was a sovereign republic within the German Confederation (1815–1866), restored by the Congress of Vienna after Napoleon had folded it into the Grand Duchy of Frankfurt under Karl Theodor von Dalberg. As the traditional site of Holy Roman imperial elections since 885 and coronations since 1562, Frankfurt held symbolic primacy among German cities and hosted the Bundestag (the Confederation's deliberative body) at the Palais Thurn und Taxis, as well as the revolutionary Frankfurt Parliament of 1848. The city's senate governed a politically moderate commercial republic whose motto was Stark im Recht (\"Strong in Law\"). After Frankfurt remained loyal to the Austrian-aligned German Confederation in the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, Prussian forces occupied the city on 16 July 1866; it was formally annexed that September and absorbed into the Province of Hesse-Nassau.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The city's boundaries as a self-governing republic are well-documented in Confederation-era administrative records; its small, urban territorial extent is precisely known.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Frankfurt am Main","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Frankfurt-am-Main"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Free City of Frankfurt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_City_of_Frankfurt"},{"label":"Britannica: Frankfurt Parliament","url":"https://www.britannica.com/event/Frankfurt-Parliament"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":n